WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:03.710 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  after weeks of demonstrations against  police brutality  nigerian forces armed with automatic  rifles  opened fire against protesters in lagos  it happened in the city's leki district  and according to amnesty international  at least 12 people were killed the  nigerian authorities  have failed to provide official figures  on the streets of africa's largest city  young nigerians are feeling  increasingly angry with political  leaders viewed as  ineffectual and out of touch here's why  [Applause]  the campaign against sars a now  disbanded branch of nigeria's police  force  has gone global it has backing from  athletes  i would say nigerian government you guys  are ashamed to the world  we've been making some tangible efforts  to try and support your protests on the  ground  and music stars like beyonce rihanna  and burner boy this is the most  important moment  in nigeria's history if this doesn't  work  then you know it's over  sars is short for special anti-robbery  squad  it was set up in 1992 to combat  armed robberies and other violent crimes  but critics accuse it  of beatings extortion extrajudicial  killings  and other human rights abuses  there have been a number of end sars  campaigns since 2016  but the latest round of protests are  different  they were sparked by a widely circulated  video  of a sars officer allegedly shooting a  young nigerian  in delta state demonstrations have been  taking place for weeks  using the social media hashtag ensars  to rally huge crowds in response to the  demonstrations  nigeria's president muhammadu buhari  said he would dissolve the sars unit  and promised an investigation into  police violence  but many protesters don't buy it they  point out that sars has been  reformed and re-badged several times  over the last few years  and they have little faith that anything  will change  many of the protesters are young in  their teens and twenties  and they feel despondent about their  future prospects  nigeria is the african continent's  biggest oil producer  but low oil prices have battered the  economy  and the fallout from the coronavirus  pandemic has left millions  on the very edge more than a third of 15  to 34 year olds  are currently unemployed with little  chance of work  what started out as a protest against  sars  has broadened into something else a  challenge to the government's authority  its right to rule over two hundred  million people  buildings have been torched a bus depot  burned  and a tv station attacked by agitators  forcing the staff to flee started  attacking us inside the studio i had to  cut off the shoe  and we couldn't go up we can't continue  the show right now i don't even know  what to do  and while a strict curfew has been  imposed  security forces have struggled to  enforce it  the birth of this political movement has  been assisted by the fact that it is  essentially  leaderless the authorities can't arrest  the people in charge  because there aren't any but that may  also be its biggest  weakness without a proper leadership  structure  this is a movement that will be very  difficult to control in the future

